  {
    "id": "cemc-gauss8-2000-24",
    "bank": "cemc",
    "source": {
      "contest": "Gauss Contest (CEMC)",
      "paper": "Grade 8",
      "year": 2000,
      "number": 24,
      "url": "https://cemc.uwaterloo.ca/sites/default/files/documents/2000/2000Gauss8Contest.pdf"
    },
    "grade": "8",
    "points": 8,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "In equilateral triangle $ABC$, line segments are drawn from a point $P$ to the vertices $A$, $B$ and $C$ to form three identical triangles. The points $D$, $E$ and $F$ are the midpoints of the three sides and they are joined as shown in the diagram. What fraction of $\\triangle ABC$ is shaded?",
    "image": "qimages/cemc/gauss8-2000/q24.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$\\frac15$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$\\frac{5}{24}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$\\frac14$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$\\frac29$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$\\frac27$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Big triangle minus small triangle",
        "body": "Let $K$ be where $PC$ crosses $EF$. The shaded region is $\\triangle APC$ with $\\triangle EKC$ removed.\n\n- $\\triangle APC$ is one of three identical triangles, so it is $\\frac13$ of $\\triangle ABC$.\n- $\\triangle EFC$ (corner triangle cut off by the midpoint segment) is $\\frac14$ of $\\triangle ABC$. By symmetry $CP$ splits it into two equal halves, so $\\triangle EKC$ is $\\frac18$ of $\\triangle ABC$.\n\nShaded: $\\frac13 - \\frac18 = \\frac{5}{24}$.\n\n**Answer: (B) $\\frac{5}{24}$**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Two pieces",
        "body": "Let $M$ be where $AP$ crosses $DE$. The shaded region splits into $\\triangle AME$ and the quadrilateral $MEKP$ inside $\\triangle DEF$.\n\n- $\\triangle ADE$ is $\\frac14$ of $\\triangle ABC$, and $AM$ (part of the symmetry line through $A$) cuts it in half: $\\triangle AME = \\frac18$.\n- $P$ is also the centre of $\\triangle DEF$, and the segments $PA, PB, PC$ split $\\triangle DEF$ into three identical pieces; $MEKP$ is one of them, so it is $\\frac13 \\times \\frac14 = \\frac1{12}$ of $\\triangle ABC$.\n\nShaded: $\\frac18 + \\frac1{12} = \\frac{5}{24}$.\n\n**Answer: (B) $\\frac{5}{24}$**"
      }
    ],
